feat(website): snap rendered HTML into dist via Playwright postbuild (#110)

* feat(website): snap rendered HTML into dist via Playwright postbuild

Fix the empty-body SEO defect. Today `vite build` ships
`<div id="root"></div>` and the `inject-route-html.mjs` postbuild
only stamps `<head>` per route. Crawlers without patience for JS
(every bot except modern Googlebot, and Googlebot itself when
under load) see no prose. The blog post at
`/blog/the-cli-is-for-the-robots` has 300+ words of content that
search engines have to discover by re-crawling with a headless
Chrome.

Fix: a Playwright crawler that walks every indexable route in
`siteMetadataRoutes` against a `vite preview` server and rewrites
the per-route `dist/<route>/index.html` so the body holds the
post-mount HTML (the head stays the metadata-injected version).

Why Playwright + not an SSG framework:
  - `vite-react-ssg` is the natural choice but its 0.9.x peer
    deps still require `react-router-dom ^6`. We're on `v7`.
  - `vike` requires migrating ~100 routes off React Router into
    its file-system convention. Too big a swing for the gain.
  - Custom Node SSR is months of work to maintain.
  - Playwright was already a devDependency. Zero new deps.

Pipeline (after this PR):
  prebuild: generate-seo-artifacts.mjs        (sitemap, robots, llms)
  build:    tsc -b && vite build              (empty-body shell)
  postbuild step 1: inject-route-html.mjs     (per-route <head>)
  postbuild step 2: snap-routes.mjs           (per-route <body>)  NEW

Settle logic:
  - Wait until the Suspense fallback ("Loading route...") leaves
    the DOM AND the root contains either a heading element or
    >200 chars of visible text. Default 20s timeout per route;
    routes that miss the window report `fallback` and don't
    overwrite the existing body (graceful degradation).
  - Concurrency 4 by default (SNAP_CONCURRENCY env override).

Verified against a clean build:
  - 277 routes captured, 0 failed.
  - Landing index.html grew from 4.5KB → 188KB.
  - Blog post `/blog/control-plane-is-the-product` → 88KB with
    all H1/H2 prose snapped in.
  - `grep -c "Loading route" dist/**/*.html` → 0 in bodies (only
    one hit in the bundled JS, the source of RouteFallback).
  - `curl -A Googlebot file://.../dist/index.html | grep -c "<h"`
    → 3 headings present in the static HTML.

CI implications:
  - `vite preview` + chromium add ~2-5 min to website-v2 build.
  - Playwright chromium binary should already be available in CI
    (record:gifs workflow uses it).

Follow-ups (deferred):
  - CI assertion that body content is non-empty per route, to
    catch a future router change that breaks Suspense settling.
  - Wire into the Cloudflare Pages deploy and smoke a staging
    URL before declaring real coverage.

* fix(snap): hard-fail on empty captures + use exitCode not process.exit

Two skeptical-reviewer fixes:

1. process.exit(1) on the top-level .catch was a hammer that
   bypassed the unfinished-but-deeper finally blocks. If main()
   threw before reaching its own try/finally, vite preview could
   be left orphaned. Using process.exitCode = 1 lets the promise
   chain unwind cleanly and any in-flight finally blocks run.

2. The exit-code logic only counted status==='fail' as failure.
   'empty' (root rendered nothing — a real regression) was
   silently classified as 'skipped' and exited 0. A future bug
   that broke every page's Suspense settling could ship an
   empty-body dist with a green CI run. Now empty counts as
   failure, fallback stays as skip (keeps the existing body),
   and ok===0 with non-zero target count is also a hard fail.

Verified by clean rebuild: 277 captured / 0 fallback / 0 empty /
0 failed, exit 0. Behavior on the happy path unchanged.

Open follow-up (not in this PR): the 'Loading route...' settle
marker is duplicated as a literal in two places. Brittle to a
future rename of RouteFallback's text. Plan: have RouteFallback
render data-snap-state="loading" and have snap-routes wait on
the attribute, decoupling settle detection from copy.

* fix(snap): graceful degrade when chromium can't launch

Cloudflare Pages build failed because its default Node image
doesn't bundle the Playwright chromium binary. snap-routes was
throwing at chromium.launch(), the postbuild exited non-zero,
and the CF deploy stayed broken.

Two layered guards:

1. SNAP_SKIP_ON_BROWSER_ERROR=1 (operator opt-in) and the
   auto-detected CF_PAGES=1 (Cloudflare Pages env). Either one
   converts a chromium-launch failure into a warning + exit 0.

2. The top-level catch also honors the same flag. If anything
   in the snap path throws (missing playwright module, vite
   preview boot failure, etc.) we degrade rather than fail the
   deploy. The cost is that empty-body HTML ships until the
   underlying problem is fixed; the benefit is that the rest
   of the site keeps deploying.

CI runners that DO have chromium (GitHub Actions test:gifs etc.)
should leave SNAP_SKIP_ON_BROWSER_ERROR unset so genuine breakage
surfaces.

Path forward to actually run snap in production (not in this PR):
either install chromium in CF Pages build (`npx playwright
install chromium` in the dashboard build command) OR move the
snap step to a GitHub Actions workflow that publishes the dist
to CF separately. Tracked in PR comment.

* fix(snap): remove snap from default postbuild

CF Pages keeps failing on the snap step. Even with the
graceful-degrade flag and CF_PAGES=1 auto-detection, something in
the snap call chain is still tripping the deploy. Rather than
hold the SSR-safety + snap tooling hostage to the CF Pages
environment, detach snap from the default build:

  - npm run build       → the existing tsc + vite + inject-head
                          flow. Cloudflare Pages stays green.
  - npm run snap:routes → the snap step explicitly.
  - npm run build:full  → build + snap, the local + CI shape.

Until snap is wired into the actual CF Pages deploy (either by
installing chromium in the dashboard build command or by moving
the deploy to a GitHub Actions workflow that runs build:full and
publishes via API), Google still sees empty bodies in production.
This commit gives us the tooling in main without breaking deploys
on the way to that wiring.

* ci(website): GitHub Actions workflow runs build:full + deploys snap to prod

CF Pages git integration can't run snap (no chromium in its
build image). This workflow takes the deploy:

  - runs on push to main affecting website-v2/**
  - installs chromium via playwright install
  - runs build:full (the existing build + snap-routes)
  - hard-fails if dist/<route>/index.html still has an empty root
    (defense in depth — refuses to deploy empty bodies)
  - deploys dist/ to the existing 'port-daddy' Cloudflare Pages
    project via wrangler-action

Concurrency-grouped so two pushes don't race. Manual
workflow_dispatch with a --branch input for previews.

Required secrets (operator action if not set yet):
  - CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN: Pages:Edit on the port-daddy project
  - CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID: from the Pages dashboard URL

The CF Pages git integration may still fire on the same push and
ship empty-body HTML first. This workflow runs afterward and
overwrites with snapped HTML — last-writer-wins outcome: full
content reaches Google.

